Z||000000||mrfixit58||12-13-2001||03:49 PM||||My 88 GT has a cranking problem. Sometimes it fires as soon as I turn the key, other time, it cranks and cranks before firing. I've already replaced the coil and that made no difference. Any suggestions?<P>Thanks in advance,<BR><P>------------------<BR>Roy  :D <BR>Just another stock Blue 87 GT   <BR>Suncoast Fieros<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG">          <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <P><B>JUST ADDED: </B>1988 GT w/ T-Tops, 5-Speed, and Performance Sound.||||reg||5
Z||000001||sqoach||12-13-2001||07:29 PM||||I don't have an answer because I have the same problem and don't know what it is, except mine is an 86 SE. Just bumping this back up, too.<P>------------------<BR>-Adam<BR><FONT COLOR="BLUE">sqoach@yahoo.com</FONT><BR>                <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigsqoach2.gif"> <BR><B>86 SE V6 - daily driver</B><BR><FONT SIZE="1"><LI>86 Sport Coupe 2.5L 5spd (soon to be sold)</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Steve Normington||12-13-2001||08:19 PM||||If you turn the key to the run position, do you hear the fuel pump whine? If not, you may need a new relay. The fuel pump kicks on for 2 seconds to start the car. Otherwise, you have to crank until the oil pressure builds up enough to kick on the pump that way.||||reg||1
Z||000003||sqoach||12-13-2001||11:26 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Steve Normington:<BR><B>If you turn the key to the run position, do you hear the fuel pump whine? If not, you may need a new relay. The fuel pump kicks on for 2 seconds to start the car. Otherwise, you have to crank until the oil pressure builds up enough to kick on the pump that way.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I can hear the pump turn on. And I replaced the fuel filter not too long ago. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Alex4mula||12-14-2001||08:24 AM||||That happens to me but only whith engine warm. In the morning it fires up at first touch. Don't know why. Have changed several parts but for other reasons. No change. I think it may be injector related (leaking?). Doesn't bother me too much.<P>------------------<BR>    Alex4mula<BR>:) Red-Original Owner & White-1/94 with PM (:||||reg||1
Z||000005||MrFieroUK||12-14-2001||10:54 AM||||Same problem here too. When the car has stood for 12 hours or more in cold weather - it fires immediately. Leave it standing warm for even a couple of minutes and it cranks hard and even requires a little throttle to get going, coughing and spluttering for a few seconds until all cylinders catch. Is this the same as your problem(s)?<P>I've checked fuel pump operation, line pressure for several minutes and operation of the cold start injector when cranking (when engine is stone cold) - do this with the pressure gauge attached to the schrader valve and you will see the pressure drop smoothly as the engine is cranked and the cold start injector opens.<P>My guess is that either the temp. sensor for the cold start injector or computer is not reading values in the correct range at anything other than stone cold thus the computer is not enriching or enleaning as appropriate, for a warm start condition.<P>I've got to fix my GT, so I'll report back when I discover the problem, if a solution doesn't get posted in the meantime...||||reg||1
